WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.180 --> 00:00:04.680
Abby finds a family and Dan Fielding&nbsp;
finally achieves his dream. But what&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:04.680 --> 00:00:08.280
will Season 2 of the new "Night Court"&nbsp;
bring us? Let's lay out the evidence.

00:00:09.000 --> 00:00:14.040
Abby Stone, of course, does not end up as the&nbsp;
Manhattan night court judge by accident. Her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:14.040 --> 00:00:18.900
father was the affable, magic-loving judge&nbsp;
Harry Stone. The late Harry Anderson played&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:18.900 --> 00:00:23.220
him for nine seasons on the original "Night&nbsp;
Court." By taking over her father's old bench&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:23.220 --> 00:00:28.500
and recruiting his former colleague, Dan Fielding,&nbsp;
to serve as public defender, she hopes to learn&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:28.500 --> 00:00:33.000
more about that time in her father's life. And&nbsp;
perhaps she'll learn more about herself as well.

00:00:33.000 --> 00:00:37.980
As the series begins, Abby is in the midst of&nbsp;
a prolonged engagement with her long-distance&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:37.980 --> 00:00:42.420
boyfriend, Rand. It's clear from the start that&nbsp;
something is out of step between them, and when&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:42.420 --> 00:00:47.040
Rand finally makes the trip from their upstate&nbsp;
New York small town to visit her in the big city,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:47.040 --> 00:00:52.380
it's even more apparent that they no longer fit&nbsp;
into each other's lives. At first, he is her rock,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:52.380 --> 00:00:57.120
providing necessary comfort as she struggles to&nbsp;
fit into her uncertain new position. But by the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:00:57.120 --> 00:01:01.740
end of the season, Abby has made the bench her&nbsp;
own and won over her cynical colleagues with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:01.740 --> 00:01:05.760
gumption and good cheer. Suddenly, it's&nbsp;
her future with Rand that is uncertain.

00:01:06.480 --> 00:01:11.760
"The Honorable Dan Fielding," the two-part season&nbsp;
finale, puts its characters at personal and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:11.760 --> 00:01:16.680
professional crossroads. As the title suggests,&nbsp;
Dan has been offered a judgeship back home in&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:16.680 --> 00:01:22.140
Louisiana, which Abby responds to in her typically&nbsp;
anxious and overexcited fashion. She plans a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:22.140 --> 00:01:27.120
massive going-away party that she hopes will also&nbsp;
function as a "don't actually go away" party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:27.120 --> 00:01:31.560
while trying to ignore the fact that Rand&nbsp;
feels neglected. The pressure is too much,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:31.560 --> 00:01:37.260
and Abby acts out recklessly. An attempt to aid&nbsp;
an elderly defendant, Louise, lands her in jail&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:37.260 --> 00:01:41.940
and facing a trumped-up felony charge thanks&nbsp;
to weaselly District Attorney Jeff Dewitt.

00:01:41.940 --> 00:01:46.680
With Dan as her attorney, Abby faces the&nbsp;
possibility of disbarment and perhaps even&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:46.680 --> 00:01:51.180
jail time for trying to help a little old lady&nbsp;
break into her boyfriend's nursing home. Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:51.180 --> 00:01:55.860
pleads with her to let him foist the blame on&nbsp;
Louise, but Abby refuses to let someone else&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:01:55.860 --> 00:02:01.440
suffer for her actions. In the end, she's saved&nbsp;
by a slew of character witnesses who testify to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:01.440 --> 00:02:06.480
her sterling character — well, that and the fact&nbsp;
that Dewitt coerced Louise into wearing a wire.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:06.480 --> 00:02:11.640
The judge declares a mistrial, and Abby returns&nbsp;
to the bench. Rand heads home to upstate New York&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:11.640 --> 00:02:16.140
after he and Abby finally come to terms with the&nbsp;
fact that they have grown apart. Dan, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:16.140 --> 00:02:20.940
says his goodbyes to Abby and to Night Court, and&nbsp;
he takes his seat on the bench in rural Louisiana.

00:02:20.940 --> 00:02:27.900
"I'm not being selfish. This is what I've&nbsp;
always wanted. I'm not abandoning her."

00:02:28.440 --> 00:02:33.540
There's a "lost in time" aspect to the "Night&nbsp;
Court" revival. The sets all look pretty much the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:33.540 --> 00:02:38.340
same as they did in the 1980s and the cases called&nbsp;
before the court are not much different. Everyone&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:38.340 --> 00:02:43.440
has smart phones, but they rarely figure into the&nbsp;
plot, and the titular tech investment opportunity&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:43.440 --> 00:02:49.020
in the episode "Past Apps" is merely the catalyst&nbsp;
for Abby's ethical quandary in dealing with an&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:49.020 --> 00:02:54.360
old fashioned con man. This is all very much by&nbsp;
design; when developing the series, producer Dan&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:54.360 --> 00:02:59.460
Rubin and production designer Glenda Rovello&nbsp;
raided the Warner Bros. television archives,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:02:59.460 --> 00:03:04.080
grabbing surviving set decorations from&nbsp;
the original series and even using the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:04.080 --> 00:03:07.920
blueprints for the original courtroom set&nbsp;
to make sure the new one was to scale.

00:03:07.920 --> 00:03:13.140
The series' nostalgic bona fides don't stop at&nbsp;
the set design, though. The revival is steeped&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:13.140 --> 00:03:17.160
in the same vaudeville energy that made&nbsp;
the original series a little old fashioned,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:17.160 --> 00:03:22.200
even for the '80s. The show's insistence&nbsp;
on warm-and-fuzzy comedy that recalls a&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:22.200 --> 00:03:27.480
more comforting era was very much a selling&nbsp;
point for star Melissa Rauch. She told Uproxx,

00:03:27.480 --> 00:03:32.040
"I sort of liken it to a bit of a time machine&nbsp;
that can take you back to that happiness."

00:03:32.040 --> 00:03:36.720
As a result, the show largely steers&nbsp;
clear of any divisive real-world issues&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:36.720 --> 00:03:40.260
regarding the criminal justice system;&nbsp;
the fact that the courtroom has barely&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:40.260 --> 00:03:44.280
been renovated in four decades is&nbsp;
arguably its sharpest political gag.

00:03:45.060 --> 00:03:50.220
Dan's move to Louisiana is a homecoming in&nbsp;
more ways than one. Actor John Larroquette,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:50.220 --> 00:03:55.560
who won four Emmys in a row for his portrayal of&nbsp;
Dan on the original series, is from New Orleans&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:03:55.560 --> 00:04:01.260
and has kept close ties to his hometown since&nbsp;
leaving for Hollywood in the 1970s. In 2013,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:01.260 --> 00:04:06.540
he was even awarded an honorary doctorate from&nbsp;
the University of New Orleans. Larroquette and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:06.540 --> 00:04:11.280
his character share a Louisiana upbringing thanks&nbsp;
to the original "Night Court" writers, who over&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:11.280 --> 00:04:16.980
the years mined a lot of humor from the idea that&nbsp;
Dan, the erudite Manhattan attorney constantly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:16.980 --> 00:04:21.900
trying to climb the social ladder, was born on the&nbsp;
bayou and raised in the possum-filled backwoods.

00:04:21.900 --> 00:04:27.060
The revival keeps Dan's Pelican State birthright,&nbsp;
but takes it easy on the redneck jokes;&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:27.060 --> 00:04:31.980
it also keeps Dan's womanizing past in&nbsp;
the past. During the '80s, Dan was one&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:31.980 --> 00:04:37.200
of network TV's most shameless lotharios, and&nbsp;
his constant pursuit of every woman in sight&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:37.200 --> 00:04:41.460
was one of the original series' most reliable&nbsp;
wells of humor. The episode that was planned&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:41.460 --> 00:04:46.260
to be the series finale features a nightmare&nbsp;
sequence in which Dan is tried, convicted,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:46.260 --> 00:04:51.240
and hanged by a courtroom filled with every woman&nbsp;
he had ever slept with. Three decades on, however,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:51.240 --> 00:04:56.340
Dan is a changed man; when Abby knocks on his&nbsp;
door in the pilot episode he's a grieving widower,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:04:56.340 --> 00:05:01.320
the kind of man who can no longer see himself&nbsp;
being with any woman, much less every woman.

00:05:01.980 --> 00:05:06.240
That first scene between Larroquette and&nbsp;
Rauch in the pilot episode is heavy with&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:06.240 --> 00:05:11.460
grief — almost too heavy, but that's a burden&nbsp;
that "Night Court" has had to carry from nearly&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:11.460 --> 00:05:16.320
the beginning. The show's early seasons were&nbsp;
marked by the deaths of actresses Selma Diamond&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:16.320 --> 00:05:22.260
in 1985 and her replacement Florence Halop&nbsp;
just a year later. Abby's grief over her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:22.260 --> 00:05:26.640
father and connection to Dan as a surrogate&nbsp;
forms the emotional backbone of the revival,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:26.640 --> 00:05:31.140
given extra poignance by the real-life&nbsp;
death of star Harry Anderson in 2018.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:31.140 --> 00:05:35.700
The show mourns not just a beloved&nbsp;
character, but a beloved actor as well.

00:05:35.700 --> 00:05:39.840
For as much weight as Anderson's death is&nbsp;
given on the show, however, other recent&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:39.840 --> 00:05:44.880
cast deaths are not mentioned at all. 2021&nbsp;
saw the deaths of both Charles Robinson,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:44.880 --> 00:05:49.980
who played longtime officer of the court Mac,&nbsp;
and Markie Post, who joined the show as public&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:49.980 --> 00:05:55.080
defender Christine Sullivan in Season 3. It's&nbsp;
possible that their characters' absence will be&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:55.080 --> 00:05:59.640
commemorated in Season 2, but it's possible&nbsp;
that in the world of "Night Court," Mac and&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:05:59.640 --> 00:06:04.800
Christine are still alive. The 1992 finale saw&nbsp;
Christine leaving the public defenders' office&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:04.800 --> 00:06:09.300
for a nascent political career, while&nbsp;
Mac tendered his resignation in order&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:09.300 --> 00:06:13.980
to fulfill his dream of becoming a film director.&nbsp;
It could very well be that Christine and Mac are&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:13.980 --> 00:06:18.000
simply too busy in Washington and Hollywood,&nbsp;
respectively, to show up to court anymore.

00:06:18.840 --> 00:06:24.720
The untimely deaths of Anderson, Robinson,&nbsp;
and Post cast a pall over the revival series.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:24.720 --> 00:06:30.180
After all, half the fun of legacy sequels&nbsp;
like this is to bring back the old cast for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:30.180 --> 00:06:34.140
one last hurrah. Other than Larroquette,&nbsp;
there's hardly any original cast left,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:34.140 --> 00:06:39.180
but the show kept one trick up its sleeve. In&nbsp;
the final moments of the finale, Dan takes yet&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:39.180 --> 00:06:43.620
another case of a soon-to-be bride arrested&nbsp;
for fighting in the French Quarter during her&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:06:43.620 --> 00:06:47.760
bachelorette party. But when he looks up at&nbsp;
the defendant, he sees a very familiar face:

00:06:47.760 --> 00:06:55.500
"Fielding? They made you a&nbsp;
judge? Take me back to jail."

00:06:55.500 --> 00:07:00.420
Yes, it's former night court bailiff Roz&nbsp;
Russell, played by comedian Marsha Warfield.

00:07:00.420 --> 00:07:05.400
Warfield joined the cast of the original&nbsp;
series in 1986 after Florence Halop died&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:05.400 --> 00:07:09.300
earlier that year. The wisecracking&nbsp;
Roz was quickly a fan favorite,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:09.300 --> 00:07:14.940
and her verbal sparring with Dan often cut the&nbsp;
egotistical lawyer down to size. In an interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:14.940 --> 00:07:19.680
with "Variety" published after the season finale&nbsp;
aired, Warfield spoke to the bittersweet feeling&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:19.680 --> 00:07:24.900
of being back on that set after so many years,&nbsp;
and without so many of her colleagues. Overall,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:24.900 --> 00:07:28.980
though, Warfield was happy to be reunited&nbsp;
with Larroquette and to meet the new&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:28.980 --> 00:07:34.020
generation — especially Lacretta, the new bailiff&nbsp;
in town. Warfield said of their conversation,

00:07:34.020 --> 00:07:38.280
"I told her, 'just do you, baby. Just be&nbsp;
you and enjoy it and enjoy the ride.'"

00:07:38.280 --> 00:07:39.540
"Welcome to Night Court."

00:07:40.080 --> 00:07:45.120
The last two scenes of the finale represent&nbsp;
a new status quo for the series. Abby,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:45.120 --> 00:07:49.980
newly separated from Rand and exonerated&nbsp;
on her felony charge, returns to the bench&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:49.980 --> 00:07:55.260
reinvigorated and ready to hear the first of&nbsp;
two hot dog-related cases. Dan, meanwhile,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:07:55.260 --> 00:08:00.120
has achieved a long time career goal and has&nbsp;
become a judge himself, presiding over case&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:00.120 --> 00:08:04.920
after case of drunken New Orleans brawls. If&nbsp;
Larroquette remains a regular on the show,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:04.920 --> 00:08:09.960
it seems unlikely that his Louisiana interlude&nbsp;
will last very long into Season 2. However,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:09.960 --> 00:08:14.880
it's possible that sending Dan off to his dream&nbsp;
job is the show's way of bidding farewell to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:14.880 --> 00:08:18.000
the character, with Larroquette in&nbsp;
a reduced role for the next season.

00:08:18.000 --> 00:08:23.160
The two-part finale also spends a fair amount&nbsp;
of time with Gurgs and her career ambitions.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:23.160 --> 00:08:27.960
Despondent over failing the Bailiff supervisors&nbsp;
exam, she attends Dan's going away party,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:27.960 --> 00:08:32.220
where her facility with remembering names&nbsp;
and faces impresses a representative from&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:32.220 --> 00:08:35.520
Scotland Yard. Gurgs is invited&nbsp;
to a training program in London,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:35.520 --> 00:08:39.060
but after coming to Abby's aid as&nbsp;
a character witness in her trial,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:39.060 --> 00:08:44.220
the possibility of her moving overseas is dropped&nbsp;
and never mentioned again. Did Gurgs really give&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:44.220 --> 00:08:49.680
up the opportunity of a lifetime to help out Abby,&nbsp;
or will her London adventures be seen in Season 2?

00:08:50.340 --> 00:08:54.240
NBC renewed the series for a&nbsp;
second season in February 2023,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:54.240 --> 00:08:58.320
just a few weeks after its surprisingly&nbsp;
strong premiere. In a March interview&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:08:58.320 --> 00:09:03.060
with "Collider," Melissa Rauch confirmed that&nbsp;
the writers' room had recently convened for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:03.060 --> 00:09:07.680
Season 2 and were working on the next batch of&nbsp;
scripts. No premiere date has been set just yet,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:07.680 --> 00:09:13.380
but the expectation was that "Night Court" would&nbsp;
return for a full season sometime in Fall 2023.

00:09:13.380 --> 00:09:19.680
The WGA strike, however, may have changed&nbsp;
those plans. In May 2023, the Writers Guild&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:19.680 --> 00:09:23.880
of America called a general strike over the&nbsp;
lack of compensation for television writers,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:23.880 --> 00:09:28.200
bringing most film and television production&nbsp;
to a halt. While some shows are continuing&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:28.200 --> 00:09:32.820
to film during the strike without the input&nbsp;
of their writers, many others have chosen to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:32.820 --> 00:09:37.440
shutter production until the strike is resolved,&nbsp;
including "Night Court." Depending on how long the&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:37.440 --> 00:09:41.100
strike lasts and how far into production&nbsp;
Season 2 was when the strike was called,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:41.100 --> 00:09:46.440
the show may not premiere until later&nbsp;
in the Fall, or perhaps Winter of 2024.

00:09:46.440 --> 00:09:51.780
Melissa Rauch, who is a producer on the show&nbsp;
along with her husband Winston, is expected to&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:51.780 --> 00:09:57.240
return for the second season, along with India&nbsp;
de Beaufort, Kapil Talwalkar, and Lacretta.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:09:57.240 --> 00:10:01.980
Larroquette, who was also credited as a producer&nbsp;
on the pilot episode, is a little more in doubt.

00:10:01.980 --> 00:10:06.240
"Rand, Dan is leaving, but it's because&nbsp;
I helped him, gave his life new meaning,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:06.240 --> 00:10:07.170
et cetera, et cetera."

00:10:07.170 --> 00:10:13.020
After the finale aired, Marsha Warfield confirmed&nbsp;
to "TVLine" that she will be returning as Roz for&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:13.020 --> 00:10:17.940
at least one episode of Season 2, so it stands&nbsp;
to reason that we will see Dan at the same time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:17.940 --> 00:10:22.020
though it's possible that Larroquette&nbsp;
may not return as a regular cast member.

00:10:22.620 --> 00:10:26.160
There's one major character from the&nbsp;
original series that we're waiting&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:26.160 --> 00:10:31.320
to see. Richard Moll's goofball bailiff Bull&nbsp;
Shannon was one of the show's breakout stars,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:31.320 --> 00:10:35.340
a kooky simpleton with a heart of gold. In the&nbsp;
two-parter that was planned as the original&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:35.340 --> 00:10:39.840
series finale, Bull is convinced that a pair&nbsp;
of little old men seen around the courthouse&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:39.840 --> 00:10:44.460
were in fact space aliens stalking him&nbsp;
for nefarious purposes. In the end,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:44.460 --> 00:10:49.920
his suspicions are confirmed. The little old men&nbsp;
really are aliens from the planet Jupiter and have&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:49.920 --> 00:10:54.660
come to recruit Bull to grab all the things on&nbsp;
the planet that the aliens are too short to reach.&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:10:54.660 --> 00:10:59.100
Bull accepts his destiny and disappears&nbsp;
from Harry's office in a flash of light.

00:10:59.100 --> 00:11:04.380
30 years later, Bull is apparently still on&nbsp;
his interplanetary adventure. No mention of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:04.380 --> 00:11:09.300
the character was made in the revival season, and&nbsp;
Moll himself has long been hostile to the idea of&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:09.300 --> 00:11:14.880
returning to the show. In 2013, when cornered&nbsp;
by a TMZ paparazzo in an L.A. parking garage,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:14.880 --> 00:11:19.620
he flatly stated that if any "Night Court"&nbsp;
reunion or reboot were to happen, he would,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:19.620 --> 00:11:23.880
quote, "run in the other direction."&nbsp;
Still, a lot can happen in a decade's time,&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:23.880 --> 00:11:28.560
and it's not unheard of for actors to be&nbsp;
lured back to beloved roles. Could Bull&nbsp;&nbsp;

00:11:28.560 --> 00:11:33.540
pop up in Season 2 of the "Night Court" revival?&nbsp;
The answer, so to speak, is written in the stars.

